Re-check any code path where `DestroyRef.onDestroy` may run after the view was destroyed. Previously `storeLViewOnDestroy` threw `NG0911: View has already been destroyed.`; now the callback fires immediately and a noop unregister function is returned.

**Before/after:**
```diff
   override onDestroy(callback: () => void): () => void {
-    storeLViewOnDestroy(this._lView, callback);
-    return () => removeLViewOnDestroy(this._lView, callback);
+    const lView = this._lView;
+    if (isDestroyed(lView)) {
+      callback();
+      // Return a "noop" callback: nothing was stored on the `lView`.
+      return () => {};
+    }
+
+    storeLViewOnDestroy(lView, callback);
+    return () => removeLViewOnDestroy(lView, callback);
   }
```

